A. 7.2 MeV, 32.8 MeV
B. 4.12 MeV, 29.8 MeV
C. 2.84 MeV, 26.8 MeV
D. 3.52 MeV, 20.8 MeV

Solve the question. At the end, write exactly one final line in this format and do not write anything after it:
Answer: <letter>
QUESTION_PROMPT_END
MODEL_OUTPUT_BEGIN bytes=855
Two-body decay: KE_mu = (mπ² + mμ² − mν²)/(2mπ) − mμ = (mπ − mμ)²/(2mπ) since mν=0.

mπ − mμ = 33.9 MeV. (33.9)² = 1149.21. Divided by 279.2 = 4.116 MeV. So KE_mu ≈ 4.12 MeV.

KE_ν = Q − KE_mu = 33.9 − 4.12 = 29.78 ≈ 29.8 MeV.

Answer B.</think>**Two-body decay at rest:** π⁺ → μ⁺ + ν, with m_π = 139.6 MeV, m_μ = 105.7 MeV, m_ν = 0.

**Available energy (Q value):**
Q = (m_π − m_μ)c² = 139.6 − 105.7 = 33.9 MeV

**Kinetic energy of the muon** (from two-body decay kinematics, using momentum conservation p_μ = p_ν):

KE_μ = [(m_π − m_μ)²]/(2m_π) = (33.9)²/(2 × 139.6) = 1149.21/279.2 ≈ 4.12 MeV

**Kinetic energy of the neutrino:**

KE_ν = Q − KE_μ = 33.9 − 4.12 = 29.78 ≈ 29.8 MeV

So the kinetic energies are 4.12 MeV (muon) and 29.8 MeV (neutrino), matching choice B.

Answer: B
MODEL_OUTPUT_END

