For a magnetic field \vec{B}, parallel to Oz, with small values of m (orbital magnetic quantum number) and B=1T, compare the order of magnitude of the paramagnetic coupling term with transition energy \Delta E of Hydrogen atom with wavelength \lambda=0.4861\mu m.

PS: for the maths use an online LaTeX editor.

Choices:
A. \left\langle H\right\rangle =\Delta E
B. \left\langle H\right\rangle \ll\Delta E
C. \left\langle H\right\rangle \gg\Delta E
D. \left\langle H\right\rangle >\Delta E

Solve the question. At the end, write exactly one final line in this format and do not write anything after it:
Answer: <letter>
QUESTION_PROMPT_END
MODEL_OUTPUT_BEGIN bytes=954
Paramagnetic term: μ_B B ≈ 9.27e-24 J ≈ 5.8e-5 eV. ΔE = hc/λ ≈ 1240 eV·nm / 486.1 nm ≈ 2.55 eV. So paramagnetic term ≪ ΔE. Answer B.</think>**Paramagnetic coupling term:**

The paramagnetic term in the Hamiltonian is:

$$\langle H\rangle = \mu_B B$$

with the Bohr magneton:

$$\mu_B = \frac{e\hbar}{2m_e} \approx 9.27\times 10^{-24}\ \text{J} \approx 5.79\times 10^{-5}\ \text{eV}$$

For $B = 1\ \text{T}$:

$$\langle H\rangle = \mu_B B \approx 9.27\times 10^{-24}\ \text{J} \approx 5.8\times 10^{-5}\ \text{eV}$$

**Transition energy:**

$$\Delta E = \frac{hc}{\lambda} = \frac{1240\ \text{eV·nm}}{486.1\ \text{nm}} \approx 2.55\ \text{eV}$$

**Comparison:**

$$\frac{\langle H\rangle}{\Delta E} \approx \frac{5.8\times 10^{-5}\ \text{eV}}{2.55\ \text{eV}} \approx 2.3\times 10^{-5}$$

So the paramagnetic coupling term is about 4–5 orders of magnitude smaller than the transition energy:

$$\langle H\rangle \ll \Delta E$$

Answer: B
MODEL_OUTPUT_END

