Answer: D.</think>## Setting Up

**Reaction 1** gives the Gibbs free energy of formation of Fe₂O₃:

$$2Fe(s) + \tfrac{3}{2}O_2(g) \rightarrow Fe_2O_3(s), \quad \Delta G^\circ_f(Fe_2O_3) = -177 \text{ kcal/mol}$$

**Reaction 2:**

$$4Fe_2O_3(s) + Fe(s) \rightarrow 3Fe_3O_4(s), \quad \Delta G^\circ = -19 \text{ kcal}$$

## Applying Hess's Law

$$\Delta G^\circ_{rxn} = 3\,\Delta G^\circ_f(Fe_3O_4) - 4\,\Delta G^\circ_f(Fe_2O_3)$$

$$-19 = 3x - 4(-177)$$

$$-19 = 3x + 708$$

$$3x = -727$$

$$x = -242.33 \text{ kcal/mol}$$

## Conclusion

The Gibbs free energy of formation of Fe₃O₄ is approximately **−242.3 kcal/mol**, matching choice D.

Answer: D
